-### Parameters
-*   $\lambda_1$: Balances L1 and D-SSIM losses. Default value is 0.2 [S1].
-*   $\lambda_2$: Weight for identity regularization. Follows cosine annealing, warming up linearly from 0 to 0.3 in the first 5k iterations, then decaying to 0.2 [S1].
-*   Cell size $s$: Default value is 8 [S1].
-*   MLP Hidden Layers: Widths 128 and 64 [S1].
-*   Hash Grid Levels $L$: Not explicitly given, but typically 4 in standard implementations [S1].
-*   Feature Size $F$: Not explicitly given, but typically 2 in standard implementations [S1].
-*   Appearance Embedding Size $A$: Not explicitly given, but typically 16 in standard implementations [S1].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Blotchy Surfaces:** Without identity regularization $L_{ID}$, Gaussian primitives tend to adjust their colors instead of the appearance model optimizing, causing blotchy homogeneous surfaces [S1].
-*   **Unpleasant Colors:** The identity regularization term prevents unpleasant colors by constraining the transformation to be close to the identity transformation [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Querying the appearance model for every pixel is time-consuming; the cell-based query approach is used to minimize optimization time [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Blotchy Surfaces:** Without identity regularization ($L_{ID}$), Gaussian primitives may adjust their colors instead of the appearance model, leading to visually unpleasant blotchy homogeneous surfaces [S1].
+*   **Optimization Time:** Querying the appearance model for every pixel is time-consuming. The cell-based query approach is used to minimize optimization time by computing matrices for cells and interpolating [S1].
+*   **3D Consistency:** The method relies on 3D consistent features from hash grids to ensure 3D consistency across views. Incorrect back-projection or depth estimation can break this consistency [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The cell-based query reduces optimization time by computing transformation matrices for cells rather than every pixel. The rendering speed is not compromised as the module is discarded after optimization [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Adds memory overhead for hash grids, MLP weights, and global appearance embeddings. The overhead is minimal compared to coupled methods [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Plug-and-play, decouples global and local appearance variations, ensures 3D consistency, maintains real-time rendering speed after optimization [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires differentiable depth rendering. Performance on scenes with extreme local appearance changes may depend on the capacity of the hash grids and MLP [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Scenes with significant appearance variations (e.g., daylight changes, ISPs, local light sources) where standard 3DGS produces floaters or color distortions [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with stable appearance where standard 3DGS performs well, to avoid unnecessary complexity [S1].
